nding surge on the Democratic side, could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Nick Begich III (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Nick Begich III seeks a second term representing Alaska's at-large district, a seat that leans Republican by about six points. He faces Democrat Eric Hafner, independent Bill Hill, and Libertarian Jim McDermott in a ranked-choice general election. Begich has raised $5.5 million and holds $3.1 million on hand, while Hafner has reported little or no fundraising. Hill has raised $1.3 million and is competing for the anti-incumbent lane.\n\nMost race raters classify the contest as Likely Republican, with a couple calling it Lean Republican and one rating it Solid Republican. The limited polling available, much of it hypothetical and partly from Democratic-aligned sponsors, shows Begich in the mid-to-high 40s with leads of roughly ten points in the few head-to-head readings. Prediction markets give Hafner roughly a four percent chance of winning. Begich's financial and incumbency advantages in a favorable seat make him a clear favorite, though the multi-candidate field and ranked-choice counting leave some room for surprise.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1234, "margin": -7.11}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1042, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0943, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1159, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0428, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0916, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1248, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0896, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0885, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.063, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.1006, "margin": -9.0}]}